cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Heptagon Intersection Project: a candidate computer-assisted proof that f(7,7) = 38"
message: >-
  STATUS WARNING. This repository contains a CANDIDATE computer-assisted proof
  that f(7,7) = 38. It has NOT been peer-reviewed and has NOT been reviewed by
  any expert outside the AI-assisted development process. Do not cite it as a
  peer-reviewed or settled result.
  Several components ARE now formally verified in Lean (the 38-crossing
  construction, the Lemma A counting/parity core, the 225-case endgame as a
  statement about a stored certificate, the AKR-L5 bridge, the purely logical
  inference "disjoint status sets imply H bipartite", and a Lean transcription
  of the Cerny-L2 filter predicate). AKR Lemma 7 and Cerny Lemma 2 themselves
  are NOT formalized: they enter every Lean statement as explicit named
  hypotheses, never as axioms. The finite reduction has been independently
  reimplemented in a second language, except for its final avoiding-pair step.
  But f(7,7) = 38 is NOT a Lean theorem: the published geometric lemmas remain
  external dependencies, and the interpretation of AKR Observation 6 / Lemma 7
  is still an open review item. If you refer to this work, describe it as a
  candidate computer-assisted proof undergoing verification, and cite the
  specific commit you examined. See docs/verification-status.md.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Jayden
    family-names: Lin
    email: lin.jayd@northeastern.edu
    affiliation: Northeastern University
repository-code: "https://github.com/NewYorkImperialist/heptagon-intersections"
abstract: >-
  Computational materials, proof notes, Lean formalization skeleton and full
  research provenance for an investigation of f(7,7), the maximum number of
  boundary intersections of two simple heptagons and the smallest open case of
  the Dillencourt-Mount-Saalfeld conjecture. The repository contains an explicit
  verified 38-crossing lower-bound construction, a new counting lemma
  ("Lemma A"), an exhaustive classification of the 16 exceptional simple
  heptagons (14 classes), and a finite elimination of the I = 40 case
  conditional on published lemmas of Cerny et al. (2003) and
  Ackerman-Keszegh-Rote (2022). Substantial portions of the work were produced
  with large-language-model assistance; see docs/ai-disclosure.md.
keywords:
